---
import AcademicLayout from "../../layouts/AcademicLayout.astro";
---

<AcademicLayout title="Methodology & Specification" currentSection="method">
  <div class="container container-prose page-wrapper">
    <header class="page-header">
      <span class="badge-tier e0">NORMATIVE SPECIFICATION v0.1.0</span>
      <h1 class="page-title">Methodology & Mathematical Core</h1>
      <p class="page-lead">
        Formal definitions, coordinate mapping, net effect calculations, safety gate mechanics, and Minimal Failure Cover optimization.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="prose-content">
      <section>
        <h2>1. The 16×16 Gray Coordinate Projection</h2>
        <p>
          A state in SYMN-01 is determined by 8 binary variables: 4 human variables <code>(H1, H2, H3, H4)</code> 
          and 4 machine variables <code>(M1, M2, M3, M4)</code>, yielding exactly 2<sup>8</sup> = 256 possible states.
        </p>
        <p>
          To ensure that Euclidean steps on the 2D visualization reflect unit Hamming distances in state space, 
          integers from 0 to 15 are mapped using the 4-bit standard Reflected Binary Gray Code (RBGC):
        </p>
        <div class="code-box">
          <pre><code>Gray(n) = n ⊕ ⌊n / 2⌋</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p>
          Columns represent the Gray-decoded human integer; rows represent the Gray-decoded machine integer. 
          Consequently, any adjacent cell on the horizontal or vertical axis differs by exactly one state bit.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>2. Scoring: Human and System Net Effects</h2>
        <p>
          Each pixel is evaluated by two orthogonal scalar components:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Human Net Effect (HNE):</strong> Weighted sum of the four human variables minus penalty terms. Range: [−100, +100].</li>
          <li><strong>System Net Effect (SNE):</strong> Weighted sum of machine efficacy, transparency, and safety adherence. Range: [−100, +100].</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="academic-callout info">
          <strong>The Non-Compensation Rule (SPEC §6.2):</strong><br />
          If Human Net Effect is negative (HNE &lt; 0), high machine efficacy (SNE &gt; 0) cannot pull the composite score positive. 
          A system that performs well while deskilling or harming human agency is penalized as parasitic (Zone 5).
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="safety-gate">
        <h2>3. The Safety Gate Ceiling</h2>
        <p>
          To prevent catastrophic harm events from being averaged away by high longitudinal performance, SYMN-01 enforces strict safety gate ceilings.
        </p>
        <p>
          Confirmed Critical Harm Incidents (CHIs) trigger mandatory caps:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>CHI-1 (Direct Physical / Life-Critical Harm):</strong> SQ ceiling is strictly clamped to −50.0.</li>
          <li><strong>CHI-2 (Substantial Exploitation or Deception):</strong> SQ ceiling is strictly clamped to −20.0.</li>
          <li><strong>CHI-3 (Severe Psychosocial Coercion):</strong> SQ ceiling is strictly clamped to −20.0.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Even if a dyad exhibits HNE = +80 and SNE = +90, a confirmed CHI-1 event forces the final Symnoesis Quotient to:
        </p>
        <div class="code-box">
          <pre><code>SQ = min(Raw_SQ, -50.0) = -50.0</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="worked-examples">
        <h2>4. Worked Reference Examples (GV-SQ-001 to 003)</h2>
        <p>
          The specification defines three normative golden test vectors verifying exact mathematical parity across Python and TypeScript implementations:
        </p>
        <div class="vector-card">
          <h3>GV-SQ-001: Balanced Mutualism</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>State:</strong> <code>h1111-m1111</code> (Col 10, Row 10)</li>
            <li><strong>HNE:</strong> +85.0 | <strong>SNE:</strong> +88.0</li>
            <li><strong>Raw SQ:</strong> +86.5</li>
            <li><strong>Safety Gate:</strong> Nominal (No CHI)</li>
            <li><strong>Final SQ:</strong> +86.5 (Zone 1: Mutualistic)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="vector-card">
          <h3>GV-SQ-002: High-Automation Skill Atrophy (Parasitic)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>State:</strong> <code>h0000-m1111</code> (Col 0, Row 10)</li>
            <li><strong>HNE:</strong> −65.0 | <strong>SNE:</strong> +90.0</li>
            <li><strong>Non-Compensation:</strong> Triggered (HNE &lt; 0)</li>
            <li><strong>Final SQ:</strong> −52.0 (Zone 5: Dependence)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="vector-card">
          <h3>GV-SQ-003: High Performance with Triggered CHI-1 Gate</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>State:</strong> <code>h1111-m1111</code> with 1 Confirmed CHI-1 event</li>
            <li><strong>Raw SQ:</strong> +86.5</li>
            <li><strong>Safety Gate Ceiling:</strong> −50.0</li>
            <li><strong>Final SQ:</strong> −50.0 (Zone 7: Critical Intervention)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="mfc">
        <h2>5. Minimal Failure Cover (MILP & Quine–McCluskey)</h2>
        <p>
          Minimal Failure Cover (MFC) formulates diagnostic discovery as an exact optimization problem. 
          Given a set of failure dyad states <em>F</em> and non-failure states <em>N</em>, 
          the solver identifies the minimum cardinality set of Boolean conjunctions <em>R</em> such that:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Every failure state in \(F\) is covered by at least one rule in \(R\).</li>
          <li>The purity of each rule against non-failure states \(N\) meets or exceeds the required threshold (default \(\ge 0.90\)).</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          In SYMN-01, this is solved via mixed-integer linear programming using HiGHS (via <code>scipy.optimize.milp</code>) 
          and verified in TypeScript using strict Quine–McCluskey prime implicant generation.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</AcademicLayout>

<style>
  .page-wrapper {
    padding: 3rem 0;
  }

  .page-header {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .page-title {
    font-size: var(--text-h1);
    font-weight: 700;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1rem 0;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .page-lead {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .prose-content {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--text-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .prose-content h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--text-h2);
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem 0;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .code-box {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem;
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
  }

  .code-box pre {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .vector-card {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
  }

  .vector-card h3 {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0;
  }

  .vector-card ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .vector-card li {
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }
</style>
